[{:seon.task/name "branches-as-in-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Production is one store and many branches; tests, checks and runs work the same way (DECISIONS: one store, many branches). Today places make their own database: `c/start!` in seon.check and seon.e2e each opens a fresh LMDB store seeded with 2,000 or 300 datoms (~0.45 s each, the open alone 213 ms where an mmap and a schema tx should cost a few ms: find out why), ~170 MB kept per bin/check run; five unit-test files each carried a copy of a fresh in-memory db helper. Correct: bin/check seeds one store per run and each step's root works on its own branch of it (branch! from the seeded commit); a step that proves recovery (durable, window, resume) kills and reopens that same store, as production would; bin/test makes one seeded test store and each test takes a branch of it through one helper (`seon.test-store/test-branch!`). bin/root runs tasks as branches of the one kept store. A separate store is a last resort and says why where it is made. Estimate first: a branch costs about one commit (sub-ms) against ~450 ms per boot; disk per check run is the seed plus each step's own writes. Measure both and put them in the commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/steps-are-branches-of-one-store seon.db-test/a-test-takes-a-branch-of-the-test-store}}
 {:seon.task/name "durable-kills-before-its-agents-flush"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `durable-write` then `durable-read` on one store, 1 run in 4: durable-read printed `:a3 resumes :dur-clone: [[\"()\" nil]]` and DURABLE FAILED; the clone's committed note was gone. Cause (`seon.check/durable-write`: the clone's `transact!` then `lib/c-kill` of root): an agent's transact! is answered when committed in memory, before the sync that makes it durable (continuous group commit, AGENTS §5), and root's SIGKILL reaches the clone through the fork server's process group before that sync. A write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush, and root's SIGKILL of the system is such an effect. Correct: phase 1's writes whose survival phase 2 asserts are made with `{:flush? true}` (or phase 1 awaits each writer's flushed fact) before the kill; a write answered unflushed and lost is the window check's business, not durable's. Deletes the race."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/durable-asserts-only-flushed-writes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-check-run-never-loses-the-seed"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction: `bin/check e2e loop` given a relative output path left the store's `:db` branch at 1,282 datoms instead of the seeded ~5,234, and every later `bin/check smoke` on it failed (`n-vars` 42, expected over 360); a fresh store passed. Also `seon.e2e/loop-run` with no directory builds its tree at `/tree` (root's merge run, `:clone 128 /tree`). Correct: find what writes to `:db` (a check may only branch from it); loop-run's directory is required by its contract, so a missing argument is invalid, not run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-check-run-leaves-the-seed-as-it-found-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "in-agent-never-orphans-its-root"
  :seon.task/plan "`seon.agent-test/in-agent` leaves its root process running when a reply times out (two roots orphaned and killed by hand). Correct: the helper's root ends when the helper does, however its forms answer."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/in-agent-ends-its-root}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-watch-waiter"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: two watch-waiters for the same job. `seon.check/await-ref` (check.clj:102, any reference) and `seon.await/await-db` (e2e.clj:20, main's connection) each add a watch, check once and deref a promise; await-ref's docstring calls itself await-db's mechanism. Audit item A17's remnant: the verdict printer and eval-records twins are gone. Correct: one waiter, `await-db` defined over await-ref (or the reverse), in the harness. It moves with `seon.main/drivers-leave-src`. Deletes: ~8 lines."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/lease-ends-with-a-verdict}}
 {:seon.task/name "attach-answers-when-its-agent-is-ready"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, 2026-10-23, from task a-test-root-names-its-store-and-main: seon.agent-test/in-agent asks the server {:op :agents} up to 300 times 0.2 s apart until the attached agent is idle, a poll (AGENTS section 9). Cause: {:op :attach} answers when the run is started, before its agent is ready, and no request waits for readiness. Correct, at the server (seon.server, the attach function): the attach answers once its agent is ready (its :ready event on root's events, tapped before the spawn, as seon.check's ready! does), or the answer carries what to await; in-agent then sends its forms after the attach answer, with no loop. An agent that ends before it is ready answers that end (a recorded verdict). Proof: in-agent with no wait loop, green beside a full bin/test; an attach whose agent dies before ready answers the death."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-stops-on-a-signal-not-on-stdin"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: seon.check/root-serve loops (read-line) until its stdin closes, and bin/root stop ends a root by closing that pipe (bin/held's TERM closes the root's stdin): stdin as a lifecycle, AGENTS section 9's mistake, with one cause for two ends (a stop and a vanished holder). Correct: the root ends on a signal (its shutdown hooks already stop it on SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP: seon.check/root-serve's end!), bin/held sends TERM to the root's process (group) at its stop, the holder keeps no pipe to the root's stdin, and root-serve parks on the stop's promise instead of reading stdin (bin/mcp's relay and the owner's `bin/root stop` unchanged). Proof: bin/root stop on a root idle for 5 s ends it cleanly (ROOT STOPPED, exit 0) with the root's stdin /dev/null; a SIGKILLed holder ends it through the starter's fifo. Needs bin/held on main."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hand-started-root-has-a-starter-or-says-it-stands"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's review of jolt/test-root, 2026-10-23: a root started without SEON_STARTER_FIFO does not die with its starter (the owner's standing root is meant to outlive the shell that started it; a root a worktree session starts by hand from its tool shell is not). bin/check and every test root always name a starter fifo (bin/held; seon.test-store/root-sh; the one-launcher check proves no test launches a root another way), so what remains is the hand start. Correct, a decision before code: either `bin/root start` requires its caller to say which it is (a standing root: --standing; any other names the starter, or is refused with the fifo it needs), or a root started from a session's tool shell inherits that session's starter. Evidence to gather first: how many `bin/root start` calls in session transcripts were never followed by a stop, and what each held (the 8 `seon lease` roots of 2026-10-23 were bin/check's, now covered). Proof: the chosen form refuses or ends an unnamed, unstanding root."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-runner-sees-its-childs-end"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (agent-view): a bin/test shard runner (test/test_runner.clj --shard, pid 44597) waited 1 h 55 m on a process test whose root process was gone: its worktree's target/seon was rebuilt under the running test. Nothing recorded that the child had ended; the shard never reported, and bin/test never ended. AGENTS §9: a wait for something that never came is a recorded fact, never a hang. Correct: a process test's waits (the binary root it starts, its agents) end on the child's exit or socket EOF, and the test fails with that reason (which process, its exit status, the last it said), so the shard reports and bin/test ends. Proof: a process test whose root is killed mid-test fails at once naming the exit, and the shard's result says so."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-process-test-whose-root-dies-fails-with-the-exit}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-store-is-made-before-roots-open-it"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 on .worktrees/first-moves and .worktrees/resumed-branch: in a fresh worktree, target/runs/test-store does not exist until the first root a test starts on it creates it (```seon.db/open!```: a store whose data.mdb is missing is created and seeded). Parallel shards start roots at once, and one finds data.mdb present but no database yet (:db-does-not-exist, the root never listens), so its test is red: seon.judge-test/a-pass-scores-each-request-once there, and three seon.agent-test roots that never answered on resumed-branch's first run. Correct: the one test store is made and seeded once, before any shard starts a root on it (bin/test's plan step, the owner of the shards), never by whichever root comes first. Proof: a fresh checkout's first bin/test of two process tests in two shards is green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-worktree-syncs-every-submodule-its-branch-pins"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session): `bin/worktree --sync NAME` moves only the submodules the worktree already has a checkout of; one main added after the worktree was made (reference-code/llama.cpp) is skipped without a word (its loop's `[[ -e $WT/$sub/.git ]] || continue`), so after merging main the worktree builds and tests without it and nothing says so. Correct: --sync does what setup does for a submodule it lacks (a worktree of the main tree's submodule repo at the branch's pin, and its nested copies), and says so; one it cannot make (the main tree has not checked it out, or lacks the pin) is named and the command exits non-zero, never silent. Proof: a worktree made before a submodule was added gets it from --sync, and one the main tree lacks makes --sync fail with its name."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-single-mode-drops-the-main-it-worked-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 (full-run-reds): `bin/check durable-write check-durable` run twice by itself hangs the second time. Cause: bin/check's single mode drops `check-<mode>` (check-durable-write) before and after, but durable-write, durable-read, window-write and window-read work on the main their argument names (check-durable), so that main and the branches made under it (:dur-a, :dur-b, :dur-c, :dur-clone) stay in target/check-store. The next run's clone! is refused (\"Branch already exists.\") and a1's merge is behind (11 keys). A full run is unaffected (its own stores under its run directory). Correct: a single mode drops the main its run works on (the argument when the mode takes one), so a mode run twice by itself is the same run; seon.check/ready! now refuses on a refused start, so this ends red rather than hanging. Proof: two successive `bin/check durable-write check-durable` and `bin/check durable-read check-durable` pairs, both OK."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "idle-waits-for-every-input-in-flight"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-25 (full-run-reds): the full run's idle was red under load (`:steps-at-idle {:render [5 6]}`, 16 bytes: one chunked `: settled` frame) and green alone. seon.check/idle now waits for the render proc's asks in flight (seon.render.proc/stats :pending, read by Flow's ping: the step that answers the last sends a settled mark). Not covered: a commit notice still on its way to the render proc (`:render-main` in the report, the render proc's :main-t behind main's t), whose step may send nothing the tab sees, so there is no mark to wait for. Correct: a barrier that answers after the render proc has taken main's commits up to a t, in order (as fork-server-caught-up does for the fork server), so the window opens on a render proc that holds main. If the red comes back, the report's :render-pending and :render-main name which case it was."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-paused-proc-takes-nothing-in-backpressure"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 18:20 in bin/prove --merge of jolt/grep-says-what-it-ignored (a change no closer to Flow than a contract's message; batch 82's merge proof had it green), load ~8 with a game on 3 cores: `bin/check backpressure` red on :ring-pushes-back alone, with :taken-before-the-pause 0 and :taken-while-paused 1 (seon.check's backpressure step: the ping after the proc's pause read 0 noise, the ping after :seon.config.check/hold-ms read 1). The flood had begun (the :started event) but the proc had taken none of it when the pause arrived, then took one while paused. Either a step in flight when the pause arrived (Flow's control channel is FIFO between steps, so a step already reading its ins completes one take), or the first ping answering before the pause took effect. Learn: core.async Flow's proc loop (reference-code, flow/impl) for when a :pause is read against a pending take; reproduce at n<=100 under load. Correct: the check measures the pause as Flow defines it (n1 - n0 at most the one take in flight, or the pause awaited as an event), never by retry. Proofs: `bin/check backpressure` green under load."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
